WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.001 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-Welcome to the show.

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:03.436 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you so much.
It's great to be here.

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:04.404 line:92%
-You are very busy right now.

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:07.040
Running for governor
takes a lot of time and effort.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I am. I was in six different
cities just this weekend.

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:11.678 line:92%
-That is a crazy thing to do.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:12.479 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:13.113 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-It's a big state.

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:14.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, I would say if you ever
told anybody

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:16.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
you were in six cities
in one weekend,

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:18.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
they would just assume
you were running for governor.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:21.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's really no other reason
to do that.

00:00:21.154 --> 00:00:21.821 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-Or a stand-up comedian.

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:23.690 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Or a stand-up -- that's true.
-One or the other.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:30.063
-So, one of things you and your
opponent, Governor Cuomo,

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:31.097 line:92%
are trying to frame yourself as

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:34.367 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the person who will most
take on Donald Trump.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:34.834 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Right.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He obviously has history of
Donald Trump

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:37.570 line:92%
being a New Yorker like he is.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
You have a little history with
Donald Trump as well.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.111
When you won an Emmy,
Donald Trump gave you your Emmy.

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:48.114 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:49.916 line:92%
-I look very pained, don't I?

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:51.584 line:92%
-Yeah. It does. You seem very --

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:54.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I look so happy
to be receiving an Emmy

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.257 size:75% position:38%
but just wish it was
from someone else.

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:57.690 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
And your other option
was Simon Cowell. You know?

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:01.628 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-But you see how Donald

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:05.265
actually doesn't want to let go
of the Emmy?

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:05.698 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:08.301
-Because, you know, he was
nominated so many times.

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:11.538
And he never had one of his own,
and in retrospect,

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:14.374
I think that maybe if I had just
let him have mine,

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:17.477
we wouldn't be in the situation
that we're in.

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:18.645 line:92%
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:20.914 line:92%
-Yes. We can all look back.

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:22.148 size:78% position:39% line:92%
We can all look back.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:23.650 line:92%
-But hindsight is always 20/20.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.453
-It does look like he is saying
to you like,

00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:29.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
"So I think this is
something we share.

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:30.690 line:92%
I'll get it on weekends."

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:31.157 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
You know?

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:33.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, one of the things you've
been talking a lot about

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:38.531
in your campaign so far is the
influence of money in politics.

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:38.965 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yes.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:40.733 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Obviously, this is something
that happens nationally.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:41.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is something
that happens locally.

00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:43.169
This is something
that happens at the state level.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:46.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
How do you go about
getting money out of politics?

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:50.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
How do you go about getting
this message across to voters?

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:51.344 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Right.

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:53.780
So, I think that obviously
the influence of big money,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.617
the influence of corporations
in politics is nothing new,

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.652 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I think
we've arrived at a moment

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:02.956
where corporations have so much
power and so much money,

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:06.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they're spending it
so plentifully on our electeds

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:09.963
that there is almost no daylight
between the corporate agenda

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:12.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
and New York state policy
at the moment.

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:14.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's, you know,
it's no coincidence

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:17.370 size:81% position:41%
that Andrew Cuomo's --
one of the industries

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.772 align:start size:94% position:6%
that donates the most to him
is real estate,

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:24.344
and we're having a tremendous
housing affordability crisis

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:24.911 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
across the state.

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:27.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think these two facts are
very, very linked,

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:31.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I think it's really
important that, for example,

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:34.721
in my campaign
I'm not accepting a single dime

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:36.956 align:start size:94% position:6%
of corporate contributions.
Right?

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.758 size:84% position:42%
-And have you found --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:44.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So that -- so that when I --
when I get to Albany,

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
I won't be beholden
to my corporate donors

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:47.667 line:92%
because I won't have any.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:50.336
-That's a very easy solution to
the problem

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.404 line:92%
of how to not be beholden.

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:51.971 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-It is. It is.

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:54.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You -- A lot of people are
giving you credit for,

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:57.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
even before the election,
pulling the governor

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.077 line:92%
to the left on certain issues.

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:01.181
You have brought up issues,
things like single payer.

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:02.916 line:92%
You mention affordable housing.

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:03.483 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
You talk about --

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:05.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Whole host of
environmental issues

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
we've got him to move
to the left on.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:07.820 line:92%
-You got him to the left.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:11.424
"The New York Times" just wrote
an endorsement, and obviously,

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:12.258 line:92%
they had to pick a candidate.

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:18.231
They gave you a lot of credit
in this piece for the issues

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:20.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you were pulling
the governor to the left on.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.702 align:start size:94% position:6%
They also said
that he had brought

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:24.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
a degree of corruption
to Albany,

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:26.506 align:start size:94% position:6%
and yet in the end
they endorsed him

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:30.276
despite making all these points
because he would be more likely

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:33.880 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be able to implement things
due to his experience.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.114 line:92%
What was it like reading that?

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:39.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, they effectively said,
right, we have a governor

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:43.323 align:start size:94% position:6%
whose administration has been
rife with corruption,

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:45.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
and if anything,
the last four years,

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:47.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
his administration
has outdone itself.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:51.931
They listed all the things that
he hasn't accomplished,

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.166 line:92%
and they effectively said,

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:56.502 size:81% position:41%
"We have an incredibly
dysfunctional, corrupt

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:57.303 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Cuomo administration.

00:03:57.303 --> 00:04:01.407
But he's our Andrew Cuomo
and we think the next four years

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:03.309 line:92%
are gonna be entirely different.

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:03.977 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Let's endorse him."

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:07.780
-That might -- I mean, because
as you read it, it seemed like

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
they were gonna definitely
endorse you,

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:11.584 line:92%
and then at the end they didn't.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:15.488
Were you like, "Wow, that was
a real twist at the end there"?

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:16.022 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:20.159 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, this is -- this is
an insurgent campaign.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:21.694 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is
a people-powered campaign,

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:26.332
and I think that so many people
in the establishment

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:30.503
are not grasping the moment that
we're in and are not grasping --

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:31.838 align:start size:94% position:6%
with Donald Trump
in the White House,

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:34.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's a really terrible moment
for our country.

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:38.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I think what voters are
trying to say

00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:42.482
to the Democratic establishment
and are saying it in volumes

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:44.083 line:92%
when you look at the victory --

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:47.053 align:start size:94% position:6%
Ayanna Pressley last night
in Massachusetts.

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:50.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you look at Andrew Gillum
last week in Florida,

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:53.893 align:start size:94% position:6%
these are --
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:56.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
These are people
who were not predicted to win,

00:04:56.696 --> 00:04:59.299 align:start size:94% position:6%
who were down by 30 points
in some cases,

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:03.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
and have marched on to victory
because voters understand

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:06.306 align:start size:94% position:6%
something the Democratic Party
leadership doesn't seem to,

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:09.108
which is that with Donald Trump
in the White House,

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:13.479 align:start size:94% position:6%
we actually have not only
a possibility

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:17.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we have an obligation
to elect leaders

00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:18.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
who will bring
real progressive change,

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:21.654 align:start size:94% position:6%
whether you're talking about
single payer healthcare,

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:24.857
whether you're talking about
universal rent regulation,

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:25.858 line:92%
whether you're talking about

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:28.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
New York becoming a leader
in renewable energy.

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:31.564
We have to elect more Democrats
this year,

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:34.867 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we also have to
elect better Democrats.

